{"name":"Tanager","hex":"#a43834","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tanager","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of numerous species of often colorful passerine birds that inhabit New World forests within the family Thraupidae."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tanager"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tanager","blocks":[{"text":"The tanagers comprise the bird family Thraupidae, in the order Passeriformes. The family has a Neotropical distribution and is the second-largest family of birds. It represents about 4% of all avian species and 12% of the Neotropical birds."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Thraupidae_Diversity.jpg/330px-Thraupidae_Diversity.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tanager"},{"title":"Tanager (satellite)","blocks":[{"text":"Tanager are a series of Earth observation satellites designed to detect and measure emissions of greenhouse gases, particularly methane and carbon dioxide. Tanager-1 is the first satellite deployed for the Carbon Mapper initiative and was launched on 16 August 2024.\nTanager-2, also developed as part of the Carbon Mapper satellite constellation, is a satellite planned for the detection and quantification of greenhouse gas emissions."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tanager_(satellite)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Plush-capped_Finch_-_Colombia_S4E1689_%2822623276893%29.jpg/500px-Plush-capped_Finch_-_Colombia_S4E1689_%2822623276893%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Plush-capped_Finch_-_Colombia_S4E1689_%2822623276893%29.jpg/500px-Plush-capped_Finch_-_Colombia_S4E1689_%2822623276893%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3